# NFOR — no-fail-open-return baseline
#
# Each line:  <path>:<lineno><TAB or two-spaces><reason>
#
# Adding to this list REQUIRES a written reason. The linter rejects any
# entry without one. Reasons should reference a triage ticket or audit
# finding so the allowlist stays auditable.
#
# Removing an entry is the *target state* for that line — the codebase
# becomes fail-closed when this file is empty.
#
# Initial baseline captured 2026-05-21 at R9-B kickoff. Established 51
# pre-existing sites; R9-B item #14 closes the high-risk subset
# (task_queue.py + train.py:301), the rest are triaged in R10.

# ── R9-B item #2 — CLOSED 2026-06-01 (R11) ─────────────────────────────
# train.py MLflow log swallow removed: extracted to _save_and_log_to_mlflow
# which RETURNS explicit (run_id, error) state; the error is persisted to
# sku_training_runs.mlflow_logging_error (migration 013) and surfaced in the
# training-runs API. No longer log-only → no baseline entry needed.

# ── R9-B item #14 — task_queue.py exception narrowing ─────────────────────
# CLOSED by #185 (2026-06-28): the degraded sync-training fallback (4 broad
# excepts doing partial run-status bookkeeping) was DELETED — the fallback now
# routes through `_training_job` (gated by ALLOW_SYNC_TRAINING_FALLBACK), whose
# error handling lives in named helpers. No task_queue.py NFOR entries remain.

# ── pre-R9 baseline — to be triaged in R10 maintenance ────────────────────
# Each of these should either get narrowed to a specific exception type,
# convert the swallow into a persisted error, or document the fail-open
# branch as deliberate (and then move out of "broad except" to a typed one).
